{# THE PLATE — the one shape every map in the product is served in.

   A flex column of two parts: the MAP BODY — the filter row and the imagery
   frame under it, which is what a plate's stated height sizes — and the LEGEND
   below them. The column is what makes fullscreen work — the plate root is the
   element that expands, and the frame grows into the space the filter row does
   not take — so a module cannot lay a map out into a card that will not grow.

   THE LEGEND IS UNDER THE MAP, NOT OVER IT (the design's `.maplegend` after a
   `.viewer`): a legend floating in the imagery covers the ground it describes,
   and on a short plate it covers most of it. It stays inside the plate, so it
   comes along into fullscreen — where it does float, because there the screen is
   all map. That is the sheet's doing; the markup is the same in both.

   The map element is UX Map's own, controller and values and all; the plate is
   what surrounds it. Both controllers are live at once: the bridge's mounts the
   map, and the atlas's listens for the events it fires.

   Rendered by Twig\MapPlateRuntime, never included by hand — a module writes
   {{ render_map(map) }}. #}
{# A custom property handed to render_map() sizes the PLATE: its height is one
   such property, and on the canvas inside it would size nothing. #}
{# THE HOOK IS THE PLATE'S IDENTITY IN A PAGE — how a filter change made in
   fullscreen finds this same plate in a fetched copy of the page. See
   Twig\MapPlateRuntime::PLATE_HOOK. #}
<div class="map-plate"{% if plateStyle %} style="{{ plateStyle }}"{% endif %} data-controller="{{ controller }}" {{ hook }}>
    {# THE MAP BODY — everything the stated height is the height OF. The legend
       is outside it, so a card that fixes a plate at 400px gets 400px of map and
       the legend under it, never 400px with the legend cut out of the map. #}
    <div class="map-body">
        {% if filters %}
            {# ONE ROW ABOVE THE MAP, inside the plate, so the chips come along into
               fullscreen instead of being left behind on the page.

               THE ROW HANDS WHAT IT IS ASKED TO THE PLATE — a submission and a
               click, because a chip is as often a link as it is a button and both
               are the same act. The markup is the module's, so the plate cannot put
               an attribute on the form or on the chips — but both events reach this
               row on their way up, and a Stimulus action hears events that bubble to
               the element it is declared on
               (https://stimulus.hotwired.dev/reference/actions). In fullscreen the
               plate answers with a swap instead of a navigation; everywhere else it
               declines and the row behaves as the markup says. #}
            <div class="map-filters" data-action="submit->{{ controller }}#filter click->{{ controller }}#filterLink">{{ filters|raw }}</div>
        {% endif %}

        <div class="viewer" data-{{ controller }}-target="frame">{{ element|raw }}</div>
    </div>{# the map body: what --map-plate-height sizes #}

    {% if groups is not empty %}
        {# A LEGEND ROW IS A SWITCH when there is a layer behind it: clicking it
           shows or hides that layer and the row says which it now is. A row
           with no layer is a key — what a colour means — and is not a control,
           so it is not a button. #}
        <div class="map-legend" data-{{ controller }}-target="legend">
            {% for group in groups %}
                <div class="grp">
                    {% if group.label %}
                        <b><i style="background:{{ group.items|first.swatch }}"></i>{{ group.label }}</b>
                    {% endif %}
                    {% for item in group.items %}
                        {# THE MARK, IN WHICHEVER OF ITS THREE FORMS THE SHAPE ASKS FOR: a
                           patch of colour, a rule for a line, or — for a live position and
                           the two states beside it — the shell's own dot, which carries its
                           state in a class because it is a drawing rather than a colour. #}
                        {% set swatch %}
                            {%- if item.shape.liveDot %}<span class="sw dot"><i class="{{ item.shape.liveDot }}"></i></span>
                            {%- else %}<span class="sw{{ item.shape.value == 'line' ? ' ln' : '' }}" style="{{ item.shape.value == 'line' ? 'color:' ~ item.swatch : 'background:' ~ item.swatch }}"></span>
                            {%- endif -%}
                        {% endset %}
                        {% if item.layerId %}
                            <button type="button" class="lay{{ item.visible ? '' : ' off' }}"
                                    data-action="{{ controller }}#toggleLayer"
                                    data-{{ controller }}-layer-param="{{ item.layerId }}"
                                    aria-pressed="{{ item.visible ? 'true' : 'false' }}">
                                {{- swatch -}}{{ item.label }}{% if item.count is not null %}<em>{{ item.count }}</em>{% endif %}
                                <span class="tog">{{ item.visible ? 'on' : 'off' }}</span>
                            </button>
                        {% else %}
                            {# A KEY CAN BE DRAWN OFF TOO — the row for a mark the plate shows
                               nowhere ("no position") is not a control, but it is not on
                               either, and reading as on would claim the ground shows it. #}
                            {# `data-atlas-shape` names the shape the key is for, so a plate whose
                               marks move after the page is drawn can find the row to recount. #}
                            <span class="lay{{ item.visible ? '' : ' off' }}" data-atlas-shape="{{ item.shape.value }}">{{- swatch -}}{{ item.label }}{% if item.count is not null %}<em>{{ item.count }}</em>{% endif %}</span>
                        {% endif %}
                    {% endfor %}
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</div>
